<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Liburnians</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 35 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Seafaring Illyrian people of the northern Dalmatian coast and islands whose fast galley, the liburna, was copied by the Roman navy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Their culture can be traced from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and from the 6th century BCE Greek sources place the Liburnians on the coast and islands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasa-river">Raša</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krka-river">Krka</a> rivers in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. Iron Age hillforts and cemeteries such as Nin show a distinct culture already formed, with wide maritime contacts. Before Corinthian settlers took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corcyra</a> in 733 BCE, Liburnians are said to have held the island.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in fortified hill settlements grouped around centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Iader</a> (Zadar), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nin">Aenona</a> (Nin) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skradin">Scardona</a>, with no single king recorded. Sea trade and raiding made them a power on the Adriatic, and their light two-banked galley became a standard warship. Greek writers claimed women held unusual authority among them. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jaipur-state">Amber</a> and Italian goods passed through their ports.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Greek colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-dalmatia">Issa</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/illyrian-kingdom">Illyrian Kingdom</a> cut back their sea power. They came under Roman control in the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE and were finally subdued during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/octavians-illyrian-campaign">Octavian's Illyrian campaign</a> of 35 BCE. Their towns became Roman municipia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 800 BCE – 35 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:43.78%;width:15.22%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 35 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Liburnian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Liburnian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liburni</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-418&amp;focus=liburnians" aria-label="Liburnians on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5068 556 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Liburnians lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5388 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5388 812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Croatia. <a href="/atlas?year=-418&amp;focus=liburnians">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

